# CI Performance Report: 
Analyzed 50 runs, 2026-03-01 to 2026-04-01.

## Priority Findings

- **[FLAKY]** CI: 15% failure rate (15/100 runs), 3 retried (+4 extra attempts)
- **[COST]** CI: 50 billable mins/day (500 total)

## Workflow Summaries

| Workflow | Runs | Median | P95 | Queue | Total | Volatility |
|----------|------|--------|-----|-------|-------|------------|
| CI | 50 | 5m | 7m | - | 4h10m |  |

## Pipeline Structure

**CI** — 60% parallel efficiency, wall-clock 20m, total job time 35m
- build-stage: 8m (40%, 1 jobs)
- deploy-stage: 12m (60%, 1 jobs) [critical] [seq]


## Runner Sizing

- [oversized] CI / quick (ubuntu-latest-16-cores, 16 cores): job takes 45s on 16 cores — consider downsizing to save ~8x cost

## Step-Level Timing

**CI / build** (50 runs):
- docker build: median 2m, p95 4m (66% of job) [3.8x volatile]
- checkout: median 10s, p95 15s (5% of job)


## Suggested Investigations

- In "CI", stage "deploy-stage" waits for the previous stage — running them in parallel could save up to ~8m per run (verify job dependencies first)
- "CI" is a high-cost workflow (50 mins/day) -- check for flaky tests, cache misses, or resource contention
- "build" has 5 outliers (worst 15m) -- step "docker build" is 3.8x volatile and likely the cause
- "CI" has 15% failure rate

## Glossary

- volatility: p95/median duration ratio per series — stable <1.3, variable 1.3-2x, spiky 2-3x, volatile >=3x
- persistence: persistent = the shift held for the rest of the window; transient = it reverted; inconclusive = too few runs after the change to judge
- q_value: false-discovery-rate adjusted p-value across all change points in this report; treat q > 0.05 as noise
- billable minutes: per-job wall clock rounded up to whole minutes times the runner multiplier; self-hosted runners bill 0

